  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper pattern this week can be characterized as zonal flow \naloft with progressive, weak shortwave troughs traversing\nthrough the flow. This will offer opportunities for periods of \nrain lasting into the weekend. This will also keep temperatures \nnear average through at least mid next week.\n\nA weak cold front moves into our area from Canada tonight into\nThursday morning. This will drive tonight's fog potential within\neastern ND and the northern Red River Valley.\n\n...Rain chances ahead...\n\nZonal flow aloft with entrance region of an upper jet is being\nobserved over the Northern Plains into Upper Midwest. This is \nallowing a weak and progressive shortwave trough/upper ascent to\nmigrate west to east across our area this afternoon into evening.\nWhile synoptic forcing is expected to remain weak and moisture \nreturn meager, a slight increase in instability is accompanying \nthis forcing/moisture. This will keep the possibility for weak \nthunderstorms this afternoon into early evening in the southern \nRed River Valley into west-central MN. Very little rainfall \namounts are expected with this, despite the convective behavior.\n\nOn Friday, a rather robust banded-rain event is expected with\nstrong forcing from frontogenesis fed by rich moisture and\ninstability. Current guidance keeps majority of this rainfall\n(in excess of 3 inches) south of our area, within eastern SD \ninto southern/central MN. \n\nHowever, given the majority of this forcing driven by mesoscale\nprocesses like fgen and ripe instability, confidence in \nlocation is not great. Vast majority of guidance keeps the\nlion's share of this rainfall south of our area, however a \nsmall cluster of guidance does allow focus of forcing for ascent\nand moisture northward toward the SD border into west-central \nMN Friday. Thus, there remains potential for this banded heavy \nrainfall to eek into locations near the SD border into west- \ncentral MN. Should this occur, aforementioned locations are \nliable to see 1 inch of rainfall. Even with a further north \nevolution, the chance for 3 inches or more is very low.\n\nInto this weekend - what was once looking like a more \nconsolidated shortwave trough is progged to translate across our\nregion looks more disorganized and weak. Thus, a lessening trend\nis being noticed in the forecast regarding the chance for\nwidespread rainfall this weekend. There is still a chance for\nrain, just not nearly as widespread as previously forecast.\n\n...Fog potential tonight into early Thursday...\n\nConfidence is medium in the occurrence of fog somewhere within\neastern ND, although confidence greatly diminishes where this\noccur with more precision. Driving the fog production is a weak\ncold front moving into ND from Canada, bringing with it ground-\nscraping low clouds behind the front. Confidence remains low in\nmore precise locations, with uncertainty mainly stemming from\nquestions on influence of mid level cloud cover.",
